{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790535068,"nonce":"879oJoDamZznHDRpbL9L6e6TJrM=","title":"wasm AOT: isolates as fresh instances of the program, channels over host imports","message":"The isolate module is registered only with the host feature, so a wasm AOT program has no Isolate or Channel. d51592b covers the browser build (the embedded runtime); this issue is the AOT program's side of the same model: an isolate is a heap of its own, and values cross by copy.\n\nDesign:\n1. An isolate is a new instance of the same .wasm. A fresh instance has fresh linear memory, so its heap is its own, and it runs the same compiled code. In the browser it is a Worker spawned through the page; under wasmtime or a native host it is a thread with its own instance.\n2. Entry. Export wlift_isolate_run(vm, module, arg). It installs the named module and its dependencies (the bootstrap already goes module by module) and runs its body, with arg as IsolateCore.arg.\n3. The build has to include isolate modules. Isolate.spawn(\"worker\") names a module by string, and walk_imports follows only import statements, so \"worker\" is never compiled. Either the walker picks up string-literal Isolate.spawn targets, or the hatchfile lists isolate entry modules. A spawn of a module that was not compiled fails with a clear error.\n4. Channels. Xfer gets a byte encoding. Transport goes through a few host imports (spawn, send, receive, join), the way ash_host_dlopen/dlsym are forwarded, so each host brings its own:\n   - Cross-origin isolated browser page: channel queues live in a SharedArrayBuffer separate from every instance's memory, and receive/join block with Atomics.wait in Workers.\n   - Otherwise: postMessage, with receive/join parking the fiber. That needs the scheduler to return to the event loop (d51592b items 2-3).\n\nConsidered and set aside: running an isolate on wlift.js's embedded runtime, with a SharedArrayBuffer shared between it and the AOT program.\n- The two builds each have their own allocator and collector, so a shared buffer could only safely carry the channel queues, not a heap.\n- The isolate's code would run interpreted, in a different runtime build.\n- The program would have to ship the embedded runtime and the module's source.\n- wlift.js exists only in the browser, while wasm AOT also targets wasmtime and Ash's page host.\n\nThe embedded runtime could still serve as a fallback for a module the build did not compile.","files":null}]}